  I have *never* noticed problems with the "tougher nasties."  
Quite the reverse.  Remember the monsters may be tougher, but 
your character is *definitely* tougher.  

  It's not a question of absolute monster difficulty, but of the 
relative strength of your character to the monsters.  And proper 
preparation can make a XP level 30 wizard an almost unstoppable 
killing machine.  (Choked on a wraith corpse in Moloch's Sanctum
indeed.)

  Those extra skill slots also come in handy for advancing in the
various spell schools.  (Why play a wizard if not to cast 
spells?)

  The wizard class's special spell, Magic Missile, does one d6 of
damage per character XP level.  At XP level 30 that averages 48Hp
worth of damage.  Get your enemies in a line, or between you and 
a reflecting surface and much fun can be had.  

  (For a lawful knight with the Magic Mirror of Merlin the damage
of the Magic Missile spell is doubled.  A lawful knight with the 
MMoM at XP 15 is the equivalent of anyone else at XP 30.  And a 
knight can keep increasing the damage the Magic Missile spell 
does.  I tell you, between #jumping, applying the lance at range
(where it won't break) and the double Magic Missile damage, a 
knight looks very powerful.  Even compared to a *wizard*.)


  How good is averaging 48Hp worth of damage per hit? (NOT per 
cast, per hit.  Remember, it's easy to get multiple hits per
casting by using bounces and/or lining up monsters.)

